.TH ORO-WINDOW-BACK 3 "" "Oro Runtime" "Oro Runtime API Manual"
.SH NAME
oro:window/back \- JavaScript API module
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
import * as module from 'oro:window/back'
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Android and Linux application Back interception for the current interactive document. Native IME dismissal and fullscreen exit take priority. An unhandled request follows WebView history, closes a secondary window or backgrounds the root. Linux maps Alt+Left and dedicated Back keys to this ordering. It exits native fullscreen first, then uses the handler/history, closes a secondary window or minimizes the root. Escape and ordinary editing keys retain their GTK/IME behavior.
.SH REFERENCE
.TP
\fBsetHandler(handler, options)\fR
Installs one handler for this document. Return true to consume Back, or false to use native navigation. Promise results are accepted within 750 ms. Throws, missing replies and stalled renderers use native navigation. Navigation or renderer replacement invalidates registration; install again in the new page. Application code decides modal, drawer and router priority. Gesture progress remains available through the existing backgesture event. The original live document can remove its registration while hidden or while its Activity is paused. Removal never grants foreground access or disables a replacement document's handler.
\fBParameters:\fR
.br
\fIhandler\fR ((request: BackRequest) => boolean \\| Promise<boolean>; required)
.br
\fIoptions\fR (BackHandlerOptions; optional)
\fBReturns:\fR
.br
\fIPromise<() => Promise<void>>\fR Idempotent handler removal.
